WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.783
Network telemetry provides insight into communications between systems, services, users, and external destinations.

2
00:00:07.783 --> 00:00:20.933
Sources may include firewall events, DNS activity, proxy logs, network flow records, secure access systems, load balancers, and selected packet or protocol metadata.

3
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:31.210
This information can reveal communication patterns, denied connections, unusual destinations, changes in volume, or unexpected relationships between assets.

4
00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:44.011
Network data often helps analysts connect activity across systems even when detailed endpoint events are unavailable, though encrypted traffic and changing architectures can limit what is visible.

5
00:00:44.011 --> 00:00:56.373
Cloud telemetry includes control-plane audit logs, identity events, workload and service logs, configuration changes, storage access, API calls, and security findings.

6
00:00:56.373 --> 00:01:05.563
Because cloud services are dynamic, collection should account for multiple accounts, subscriptions, regions, tenants, and service-specific logging options.

7
00:01:05.563 --> 00:01:11.579
Modern security programs combine on-premises and cloud telemetry to support hybrid investigations.

8
00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:25.368
Useful results depend on consistent identifiers, synchronized time, asset and identity context, and an understanding of which provider, customer, or service is responsible for each part of collection and retention.

9
00:01:25.368 --> 00:01:29.449
Shared responsibility is especially important in cloud environments.

10
00:01:29.449 --> 00:01:38.678
A provider may generate an audit event, while the customer must enable export, choose a destination, set retention, and control access.

11
00:01:38.678 --> 00:01:49.404
Service onboarding should verify these responsibilities in practice so that a documented logging requirement actually results in searchable, protected, and timely evidence.
